---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { challenges, categories, totalPoints } from '../lib/challenges';
import { studio } from '../lib/site';
import { tools } from '../data/tools';
import { allTutorials } from '../lib/tutorials';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') + '/';
const cats = categories();
const lessons = await allTutorials();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About us"
  description="What Learmux is, how the sandbox works, and how the site is built."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href={base}>Home</a><span class="sep">›</span><span>About us</span>
    </nav>

    <header style="margin-bottom:1.4rem">
      <h1>About us</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Learmux is a small publication about Termux, Android's terminal and Linux environment, and about
        the command line in general.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        Most command-line tutorials stop at listing commands. Learmux pairs each short lesson with a
        challenge that makes you use it. You read about <code>grep</code>, then you pull a flag out of a
        noisy log with it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything is designed for a phone first, because that is where Termux lives. It works just as well
        on a laptop.
      </p>

      <h2>Who makes it</h2>
      <p>
        Learmux is designed, built and maintained by <strong>{studio.name}</strong>, an independent
        research and engineering studio. We build software that teaches.
      </p>
      <p>
        The studio builds for people who have the least. The slowest connection, the oldest device, the
        smallest budget. So its work is offline-first and CPU-only wherever it can be. Learmux follows the
        same rule. Static pages, no tracking, no account, and nothing that needs a server.
      </p>
      <a class="studio-credit" href={studio.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <img src={`${base}brand/apsides-labs.svg`} alt={studio.name} />
      </a>

      <h2>How the sandbox works</h2>
      <p>
        There is no operating system behind the terminal, and nothing you type reaches a real shell. Each
        challenge declares a virtual filesystem, and your keystrokes go through a command parser written
        for this site. The same command always gives the same result. Resetting a challenge puts the
        original files back.
      </p>
      <p>
        Commands that would normally touch a network or the device, such as <code>nmap</code>,
        <code>curl</code>, <code>pkg</code> and <code>ps</code>, return output written for that challenge.
        Nothing depends on a server.
      </p>

      <h2>How challenges are validated</h2>
      <p>
        Completing a challenge is decided by explicit rules stored with the challenge, not by scanning for a
        flag string. A rule might require that you read a particular file, run a particular command, or move
        into a particular directory. Submitting the flag directly also works.
      </p>

      <h2>Numbers</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{lessons.length}</span><span class="l">lessons</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{tools.length}</span><span class="l">tools documented</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{challenges.length}</span><span class="l">challenges</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><span class="n">{totalPoints}</span><span class="l">points</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="actions" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <a class="btn btn--green" href={`${base}challenges/`}>Play the CTF</a>
      <a class="btn btn--outline" href={`${base}learn/`}>Read the lessons</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
